From ac704ed3443f66f6d8e32eb5073ce655193019b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Revulate <76570121+Revulate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:39:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf(gpui_windows): request frames only for windows with
 frame demand

The vsync thread invalidated every window on every vsync, so each window
received a WM_PAINT and ran its `on_request_frame` callback at the
display rate even when nothing was dirty. An idle, unfocused window kept
the vsync thread and the main thread awake at the refresh rate all day.

Windows now implements the frame-demand protocol GPUI already has for
macOS and the web. `frame_waker` fires when a window first becomes dirty.
`schedule_frame` is called for next-frame callbacks, present-only frames
from animation patching, running scene animations, and at the end of
every effect flush for a window that is dirty, has a pending present or
callbacks, or runs a scene animation (that last condition is added here).
Both set a per-window `FrameDemand`, and the vsync thread invalidates a
window only when it has demand that is due, consuming it then.

A frame that GPUI's frame-rate throttle defers (the inactive-window cap,
low power, thermal, a scene animation's interval) used to ask for a
retry on every vsync, so a throttled animation still woke the main thread
at the refresh rate. GPUI now offers the deadline to the platform
(`PlatformWindow::schedule_frame_after`, false by default, so other
platforms keep the retry), and Windows holds the frame until then. The
deadline replaces the demand GPUI just deferred: the effect flush at the
start of a frame request schedules every window with demand again, and
that must not make the deferred frame due on the next vsync. Later
demand (a notify, activation, the start of high-rate input, which lifts
the inactive cap) makes the frame due at once.

The backend adds its own demand where it needs a frame:

- a draw deferred because another draw was in progress (Windows can
  re-enter the window procedure mid-draw), and the GPUI callback's own
  re-entrant deferral, which now wakes the frame source;
- a resize or restore (`WM_SIZE`), being shown (`WM_SHOWWINDOW`),
  activation, a renderer resize failure, a device-loss recovery, and a
  draw or present that fails because the device was removed, which now
  also starts recovery at once instead of at the vsync thread's next
  device check;
- a touchpad gesture: Direct Manipulation only advances a gesture and its
  inertia when it is updated from `draw_window`. The viewport's status
  handler requests frames whenever it reports running or inertia (the
  status can arrive outside a draw and long after the contact). The
  window then gets every vsync; for a second after a contact is handed
  over, and for 250 ms after the viewport becomes ready (its transform
  reset runs a second cycle), it polls at about 30 Hz.

`draw_window` validates the update region before it draws, not after. A
window that keeps animating re-arms its demand while it is drawn, and the
vsync thread may consume that demand and invalidate the window before the
draw returns; validating afterwards erased that invalidation while GPUI,
still dirty, would not ask again, so the window stalled until another
effect flush or the safety frame.

A hidden or minimized window keeps its demand without being invalidated.
The window procedure tracks both states, so such a window's requests (one
per effect flush while it stays dirty) do not wake the vsync thread, and
showing or restoring it requests a frame.

The vsync thread keeps waiting for vsyncs for 250 ms after it last
delivered a frame, which covers the gaps of ordinary animations and
typing, then parks until a request, the next deferred frame or the next
safety frame. Every second it invalidates each drawable window anyway (a
safety frame), so a demand source that was missed shows up as a late
frame rather than a frozen window; the deadline also bounds how late a
parked thread notices a lost device or runs the hardware-adapter probe.
When a request (or a deadline) wakes the parked thread it invalidates at
once instead of first waiting on `DwmFlush`, whose first call after a
pause can return early and fall back to a full-interval sleep. Only a
thread that finds no other thread's sleeping mark may set its own, so a
stopped thread winding down after a GPU detach/attach cannot hide the new
thread's sleep from requests.

Demand that arrives on the UI thread after a pause is painted at once:
when the window has had no `WM_PAINT` for a refresh period, the frame
waker and `schedule_frame` take the demand and call `InvalidateRect`
themselves, so the frame is drawn as soon as the UI thread is idle. With
a request on every vsync a `WM_PAINT` was nearly always pending, and a
frame for new input came that soon; waiting for the next vsync instead
made the first keystroke after a pause measurably slower. Within a
refresh period of the last paint the vsync thread paces the window as
above, so it still draws at most about once per refresh period.

`Window::set_frame_demand_gating(false)` (`PlatformWindow::
set_frame_demand_gating`, a no-op on other platforms) restores an
invalidation on every vsync for that window, and keeps the thread
ticking while such a window exists. Gating is on by default.

Measured on one Windows machine (143 Hz display), debug builds of the
examples, per-thread context switches from NtQuerySystemInformation,
ABBA:

- `hello_world`, idle, visible and unfocused, 60 s: before this change
  the main thread switched 369 and 156 times/s and the VSyncProvider
  thread 204 and 169/s; with it, main 1.2 and 1.2/s and VSyncProvider
  2.6 and 2.6/s.
- `frame_demand --animate`, unfocused (the default 33 ms inactive cap),
  30 s: without the deadline, main 188 and 183/s; with it, 97 and 97/s.
  VSyncProvider stays at about 175/s while frames keep coming (the
  250 ms wait).
- Delay from a timer notify to its render (`frame_demand --fps N
  --report 20`, focused, measured before the immediate paint above was
  added): at 2 Hz, where the thread parks between frames,
  6.8 and 6.5 ms mean with a 4-vsync wait and a `DwmFlush` after waking,
  3.9 and 4.1 ms with frames on every vsync, 0.15 and 0.16 ms now; at
  5 Hz 5.0 and 6.6, 3.5 and 3.6, and 3.7 and 4.4 ms now; at 20 Hz 5.2 and
  4.8, 4.4 and 3.7, and 4.2 and 3.9 ms now.
- Echo dist build, keystroke in the focused composer to its pixels
  changing on screen (polling the composer region), three runs each, ABBA,
  90 keys after 1.5-2.5 s of idle and 180 keys at 110 ms intervals per
  setting: without the immediate paint, idle-then-type median 15.6 ms and
  p95 24.9 ms against 14.6 and 20.1 ms with a request on every vsync; with
  it, 13.4 and 19.4 ms against 14.0 and 20.3 ms, and continuous typing
  12.5 and 16.3 ms against 15.1 and 23.0 ms.

The new `frame_demand` example shows static content, and optionally a
timer-driven counter (`--tick`, `--fps`), a continuous animation
(`--animate`), the ungated behaviour (`--no-gating`) and a pacing report
(`--report`). Unit tests cover the demand transitions, deferred frames
and which requests override them, hidden and restored windows, an
invalidation landing during a draw (`serve_paint`), the immediate
paint and its pacing, the vsync thread's
wait, sleep and safety-frame schedule, the sleeping mark, a request
waking the sleeping thread, and a patched animated image waking the frame
source.

